1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,860
Here's what a paused frame looked like
in an early draft of one of these videos,

2
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:07,168
about tic-tac-toe, for kids around 12.

3
00:00:07,488 --> 00:00:11,733
Look at the line on the right:
4 times 3 times 2, and then 12.

4
00:00:11,967 --> 00:00:13,119
Pause for a moment.

5
00:00:13,439 --> 00:00:15,999
If you were 12,
what would you think went wrong here?

6
00:00:22,233 --> 00:00:25,604
It looks like bad multiplication,
but the math was right.

7
00:00:25,924 --> 00:00:30,702
The 12 was a running count on its way to 24,
sitting on the formula's line.

8
00:00:31,022 --> 00:00:32,196
The layout lied.

9
00:00:32,516 --> 00:00:34,628
And the reviewer
who caught it wasn't a child.

10
00:00:34,948 --> 00:00:37,785
It was an AI,
pretending to be a 12-year-old.

11
00:00:38,033 --> 00:00:39,505
Now the strange part.

12
00:00:39,825 --> 00:00:43,572
Almost everything in these videos
was made by AI agents:

13
00:00:43,572 --> 00:00:46,716
the script, the animation code,
and the checks.

14
00:00:47,036 --> 00:00:49,958
And none of them can hear the narration,
or press play.

15
00:00:50,278 --> 00:00:52,113
So how do they know any of it works?

16
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:55,176
This video covers the pipeline,
who did what,

17
00:00:55,176 --> 00:00:59,259
how work gets checked without eyes or ears,
the Chinese versions,

18
00:00:59,259 --> 00:01:02,462
what still needs a human,
and what to improve next.

19
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:06,440
It started with one person, the user:

20
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:09,476
a researcher with more papers
to read than time.

21
00:01:09,796 --> 00:01:14,430
The look comes from 3Blue1Brown,
whose videos are animated in Manim,

22
00:01:14,430 --> 00:01:16,815
where every frame is drawn by code.

23
00:01:17,135 --> 00:01:21,245
The idea for this project came
from a post by Andrej Karpathy,

24
00:01:21,245 --> 00:01:23,300
as quoted in the first request.

25
00:01:23,533 --> 00:01:25,483
The user's own words go further:

26
00:01:25,483 --> 00:01:29,016
our brain is a neural net,
and training it takes hardship.

27
00:01:29,336 --> 00:01:30,967
AI is patient, they said,

28
00:01:30,967 --> 00:01:34,228
but they were doing a lot
of cognitive offloading:

29
00:01:34,228 --> 00:01:36,803
letting the AI do the thinking for them.

30
00:01:37,050 --> 00:01:40,229
A video should be a mentor,
showing how papers connect,

31
00:01:40,229 --> 00:01:42,021
because they're never isolated.

32
00:01:42,341 --> 00:01:44,209
And video is only a first step:

33
00:01:44,209 --> 00:01:48,549
the next is something interactive,
where the learner has to make things.

34
00:01:48,783 --> 00:01:50,319
Then came four requests.

35
00:01:50,639 --> 00:01:52,439
A privacy paper from a friend,

36
00:01:52,439 --> 00:01:55,439
which the user hadn't read,
to keep the test fair.

37
00:01:55,759 --> 00:01:58,468
A tic-tac-toe video,
for a middle-school kid.

38
00:01:58,788 --> 00:02:00,346
Chinese versions of both.

39
00:02:00,666 --> 00:02:01,711
And this video.

40
00:02:03,983 --> 00:02:05,946
The team: one human, the user.

41
00:02:06,266 --> 00:02:08,484
One AI agent: Claude Code.

42
00:02:08,804 --> 00:02:12,690
Under it,
183 sub-agents in 46 workflow runs,

43
00:02:12,690 --> 00:02:14,244
before this video.

44
00:02:14,564 --> 00:02:19,386
A workflow is a script the agent writes
to launch sub-agents, each with one job.

45
00:02:19,706 --> 00:02:22,941
And tools,
like a synthetic voice for each language

46
00:02:22,941 --> 00:02:24,463
and a speech recognizer.

47
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:27,729
The user decided
what was worth learning, and for whom.

48
00:02:28,049 --> 00:02:33,844
They uploaded 36 PDFs,
gave the tic-tac-toe counts to explain,

49
00:02:33,844 --> 00:02:39,825
like 255,168 games,
and supplied the program that video teaches.

50
00:02:40,145 --> 00:02:41,319
It already worked.

51
00:02:41,639 --> 00:02:44,571
The agent only changed a zero
into the letter O,

52
00:02:44,571 --> 00:02:46,097
and rewrote the comments.

53
00:02:46,333 --> 00:02:47,805
The agent did the rest.

54
00:02:48,125 --> 00:02:50,685
It wrote the toolkit that runs the pipeline,

55
00:02:50,685 --> 00:02:53,456
and made all but two
of over a hundred commits,

56
00:02:53,456 --> 00:02:55,165
the project's saved versions.

57
00:02:55,485 --> 00:02:59,112
In under a week, work stopped
at least six times on usage limits.

58
00:02:59,432 --> 00:03:03,001
Twice, it then sat idle
for hours after the limit reset,

59
00:03:03,001 --> 00:03:04,594
until the user nudged it.

60
00:03:05,583 --> 00:03:09,894
From request to final cut,
the privacy video took about six hours,

61
00:03:09,894 --> 00:03:11,983
and tic-tac-toe just over three.

62
00:03:12,303 --> 00:03:16,868
The Chinese versions took far longer:
16 hours, and about two days.

63
00:03:17,188 --> 00:03:20,047
Drawing the final frames took
under half an hour each.

64
00:03:20,367 --> 00:03:24,050
The rest went to translating,
rounds of review and fixes,

65
00:03:24,050 --> 00:03:27,215
a subtitle tool reworked four times,
and waiting.

66
00:03:27,535 --> 00:03:31,192
And the cost on screen
is what the whole session had cost

67
00:03:31,192 --> 00:03:34,309
by October 7th,
at the public pay-per-use price:

68
00:03:34,309 --> 00:03:36,452
all four requests, not one video.

69
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:40,422
The pipeline is a chain of files:

70
00:03:40,422 --> 00:03:44,150
a paper,
a catalog entry, a digest with page numbers,

71
00:03:44,150 --> 00:03:46,932
a script, animation code, and the video.

72
00:03:47,252 --> 00:03:51,476
Every step leaves a file behind,
so you can stop, review, and resume.

73
00:03:51,850 --> 00:03:53,301
Each file gets checked.

74
00:03:53,621 --> 00:03:57,921
Agents cataloging the papers read them,
not just their names,

75
00:03:57,921 --> 00:04:01,727
and found that 4 of the 36 PDFs
were the wrong papers.

76
00:04:02,047 --> 00:04:05,653
And the privacy digest even lists
the paper's own mistakes.

77
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:10,460
In the script,
each beat pairs a show line, the picture,

78
00:04:10,460 --> 00:04:13,749
with a say line:
the exact words the voice will speak.

79
00:04:14,069 --> 00:04:17,503
The animation code reads
those words straight from this file.

80
00:04:17,823 --> 00:04:21,557
It's written for the ear:
nine factorial is spelled out in words.

81
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:25,302
So other agents review the script
before any animation:

82
00:04:25,302 --> 00:04:30,141
a sentence takes seconds to fix,
but a finished scene has to be drawn again.

83
00:04:30,461 --> 00:04:33,640
The agent wrote that rule
into its own guide on day one.

84
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:37,032
Minutes later,
it started six sub-agents building scenes

85
00:04:37,032 --> 00:04:38,952
while the review was still running.

86
00:04:39,272 --> 00:04:40,813
Eleven minutes after that,

87
00:04:40,813 --> 00:04:43,697
one review came back
with changes to the script,

88
00:04:43,697 --> 00:04:45,139
so all six were stopped.

89
00:04:45,459 --> 00:04:47,630
Writing the rule down was not enough:

90
00:04:47,630 --> 00:04:50,387
the workflow itself
has to wait for the review.

91
00:04:51,733 --> 00:04:53,056
Now the animation.

92
00:04:53,376 --> 00:04:55,974
In this pipeline, the voice is made first,

93
00:04:55,974 --> 00:04:58,880
and the animation waits for words,
not seconds.

94
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:03,210
This code says: when the narration
reaches "A hundred", show a hundred.

95
00:05:03,530 --> 00:05:05,813
When it reaches "A million", show a million.

96
00:05:06,050 --> 00:05:10,914
The voice speaks one sentence at a time,
so every sentence start is known exactly.

97
00:05:11,234 --> 00:05:13,794
Inside a sentence, the toolkit has to guess.

98
00:05:14,114 --> 00:05:17,335
It assumes every character
takes the same time to say.

99
00:05:17,583 --> 00:05:18,735
Pause and try it.

100
00:05:19,055 --> 00:05:23,258
The number on screen has 7 characters,
as many as the word example.

101
00:05:23,578 --> 00:05:24,922
Say both out loud.

102
00:05:25,242 --> 00:05:26,842
How long does the number take?

103
00:05:35,083 --> 00:05:36,267
About three seconds:

104
00:05:36,267 --> 00:05:39,520
three hundred sixty-two thousand,
eight hundred eighty.

105
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:41,334
Far longer than the word.

106
00:05:41,654 --> 00:05:46,006
So the toolkit's guess for the words right
after it lands almost two seconds early.

107
00:05:46,250 --> 00:05:47,910
The fix so far is manual:

108
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:51,134
this one scene carries
about 20 hand-set shifts,

109
00:05:51,134 --> 00:05:53,418
the biggest two point two seconds.

110
00:05:53,738 --> 00:05:55,698
A better fix is within reach:

111
00:05:55,698 --> 00:06:00,159
the online voice used for Chinese
can send the time of every word.

112
00:06:00,479 --> 00:06:04,276
But the toolkit doesn't ask for them yet,
and keeps only the audio.

113
00:06:06,917 --> 00:06:07,941
Pause and think.

114
00:06:08,261 --> 00:06:10,842
Suppose you can't hear,
and you can't press play.

115
00:06:11,162 --> 00:06:12,656
How would you check a video?

116
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:24,975
The agent's answer: turn time into pictures,
and sound into text.

117
00:06:25,295 --> 00:06:27,300
The toolkit renders a quick draft,

118
00:06:27,300 --> 00:06:30,543
and tiles a still
from every two seconds into one grid.

119
00:06:30,863 --> 00:06:34,276
An agent can look at an image,
so this is how it watches.

120
00:06:34,517 --> 00:06:38,089
Between stills, things can go wrong,
so to check motion,

121
00:06:38,089 --> 00:06:40,768
it grabs frames a fifth of a second apart.

122
00:06:41,088 --> 00:06:43,242
If they're all the same, nothing moved.

123
00:06:43,484 --> 00:06:45,425
Then it measures everything it can.

124
00:06:45,745 --> 00:06:49,783
A checker called a lint runs each scene
without drawing a frame,

125
00:06:49,783 --> 00:06:51,700
and flags anything off screen,

126
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:54,641
text that's too small,
or objects left behind.

127
00:06:54,961 --> 00:06:57,508
And the tic-tac-toe scenes check themselves,

128
00:06:57,508 --> 00:07:00,230
with 82 checks
on the numbers and boards shown.

129
00:07:02,250 --> 00:07:05,962
Next come the reviewers,
fresh agents that didn't build the video.

130
00:07:06,282 --> 00:07:07,677
They get the stills,

131
00:07:07,677 --> 00:07:11,164
the subtitles as a stand-in for sound,
and a role:

132
00:07:11,164 --> 00:07:15,221
a director, or a simulated 12-year-old,
sharp but ordinary.

133
00:07:15,541 --> 00:07:20,341
That simulated kid found real problems,
like move labels it couldn't decode.

134
00:07:20,583 --> 00:07:25,020
Round one found 30 issues,
including that 12 on the formula line.

135
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:29,006
In round two,
a fresh director re-checked each one:

136
00:07:29,006 --> 00:07:31,378
25 were fixed, and 5 only partly.

137
00:07:34,017 --> 00:07:35,809
The director caught a subtler bug.

138
00:07:36,129 --> 00:07:39,284
The scene should shuffle 4 marks
through all 24 orders,

139
00:07:39,284 --> 00:07:42,038
but the board froze
while the counter ticked on.

140
00:07:42,358 --> 00:07:44,192
The cause is a Manim pitfall:

141
00:07:44,192 --> 00:07:47,353
every move was set up
before the first one played,

142
00:07:47,353 --> 00:07:49,693
and each setup erased the one before,

143
00:07:49,693 --> 00:07:52,918
so each mark jumped
to its last spot and sat still.

144
00:07:53,238 --> 00:07:57,206
Now each move is set up only when it plays,
and the marks move again.

145
00:08:00,067 --> 00:08:02,435
Some problems only show across scenes.

146
00:08:02,755 --> 00:08:06,097
In the privacy video,
agents building different scenes

147
00:08:06,097 --> 00:08:09,624
named the same person Dan in one part
and Dev in another.

148
00:08:09,944 --> 00:08:12,846
Only a reviewer checking
across scenes could catch that.

149
00:08:13,166 --> 00:08:17,518
Another reviewer found the privacy budget
bar drawn five different ways.

150
00:08:17,838 --> 00:08:19,843
Now those scenes share one drawing.

151
00:08:22,683 --> 00:08:23,792
On to Chinese.

152
00:08:24,112 --> 00:08:28,555
The user asked to keep English terms
that would sound weird translated,

153
00:08:28,555 --> 00:08:30,619
with subtitles in both languages.

154
00:08:30,939 --> 00:08:34,988
The key rule: one Chinese sentence
for each English sentence,

155
00:08:34,988 --> 00:08:38,704
so every animation cue still
has a sentence to wait for.

156
00:08:39,024 --> 00:08:41,691
Some cues are also pinned to a Chinese word.

157
00:08:43,133 --> 00:08:45,525
Both languages share the same scene code,

158
00:08:45,525 --> 00:08:48,850
so the agent checks
that the English video didn't change.

159
00:08:49,170 --> 00:08:52,541
It compares a fingerprint of every frame,
before and after.

160
00:08:52,861 --> 00:08:57,277
A few scenes don't render exactly
the same every time, but most match.

161
00:08:57,516 --> 00:08:58,711
Then, which voice?

162
00:08:59,031 --> 00:09:02,252
The agent can't listen,
so it asked a speech recognizer.

163
00:09:02,572 --> 00:09:04,343
With the first Mandarin voice,

164
00:09:04,343 --> 00:09:07,647
the recognizer heard "Nice"
where the script said noise,

165
00:09:07,647 --> 00:09:09,889
and "Excellent" where it said epsilon.

166
00:09:10,209 --> 00:09:13,494
Another Mandarin voice
passed all 17 test terms,

167
00:09:13,494 --> 00:09:14,520
and was chosen,

168
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:18,359
though two English-first
voices scored as high or higher.

169
00:09:18,679 --> 00:09:21,623
There's no record yet
of anyone checking it by ear.

170
00:09:21,866 --> 00:09:23,829
And one subtitle flipped a meaning.

171
00:09:24,149 --> 00:09:27,276
The English narration asks
whether X always wins,

172
00:09:27,276 --> 00:09:31,679
answers with a short no,
then says a computer can do more than count.

173
00:09:31,999 --> 00:09:34,307
The Chinese subtitle said the opposite:

174
00:09:34,307 --> 00:09:36,970
that this is not something
a computer can do.

175
00:09:37,290 --> 00:09:40,213
Both AI reviewers
of the Chinese version caught it.

176
00:09:40,450 --> 00:09:41,538
Pause on this one.

177
00:09:41,858 --> 00:09:45,250
The English line is right,
and the Chinese says the opposite.

178
00:09:45,570 --> 00:09:47,276
Was this a bad translation?

179
00:09:58,317 --> 00:09:59,317
It wasn't.

180
00:09:59,533 --> 00:10:02,361
The translation
had a full stop after that no,

181
00:10:02,361 --> 00:10:04,328
but the subtitle tool dropped it

182
00:10:04,328 --> 00:10:07,341
when it merged
that short subtitle into the next.

183
00:10:07,661 --> 00:10:10,114
Tool bugs often look like content bugs.

184
00:10:10,350 --> 00:10:13,343
Another tool bug:
when putting the video together,

185
00:10:13,343 --> 00:10:15,798
the toolkit reused each scene's old clip,

186
00:10:15,798 --> 00:10:19,630
so edited scenes were quietly stitched
from out-of-date footage.

187
00:10:19,950 --> 00:10:23,598
The file dates gave it away:
the clips were older than the code.

188
00:10:23,918 --> 00:10:27,942
Now a scene is rendered again
whenever it's older than its sources,

189
00:10:27,942 --> 00:10:29,444
or its voice has changed.

190
00:10:30,734 --> 00:10:32,313
Fixes need checking too.

191
00:10:32,633 --> 00:10:37,291
For the Chinese privacy video,
reviewers raised 103 points,

192
00:10:37,291 --> 00:10:41,081
most of them polish,
and fixers made 75 changes.

193
00:10:41,401 --> 00:10:44,398
Then skeptical verifiers
checked the fixers' work,

194
00:10:44,398 --> 00:10:46,137
and made 12 more corrections.

195
00:10:46,457 --> 00:10:50,778
A Chinese passage may run
at most 15 percent longer than the English,

196
00:10:50,778 --> 00:10:52,281
or the animation stalls.

197
00:10:52,601 --> 00:10:55,417
One fixer said a passage now fit that limit.

198
00:10:55,737 --> 00:10:59,192
A verifier timed it:
fifteen point three percent longer.

199
00:11:02,233 --> 00:11:03,342
So what's next?

200
00:11:03,662 --> 00:11:06,649
The biggest gaps sit exactly
where measuring runs out.

201
00:11:06,969 --> 00:11:10,169
First, and most important:
what only people can do.

202
00:11:10,489 --> 00:11:13,513
The only human feedback
on record is the user's:

203
00:11:13,513 --> 00:11:18,553
add background music to the long videos,
and make a shorter, denser version too.

204
00:11:18,873 --> 00:11:23,353
There's no record yet of anyone checking
the narration line by line, by ear.

205
00:11:23,673 --> 00:11:26,089
Every test viewer on record was simulated,

206
00:11:26,089 --> 00:11:28,964
and there's no measure yet
of what anyone learned.

207
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:32,209
People should also revisit
the agent's own calls:

208
00:11:32,209 --> 00:11:35,955
the length of the privacy video,
the choice of Chinese voice,

209
00:11:35,955 --> 00:11:40,314
which English terms to keep,
and the small edits to the user's program.

210
00:11:43,550 --> 00:11:44,873
Second, engineering.

211
00:11:45,193 --> 00:11:48,741
The free Chinese voice
isn't licensed for published videos,

212
00:11:48,741 --> 00:11:52,830
so the Chinese versions need
a licensed voice before they're posted.

213
00:11:53,150 --> 00:11:57,523
Timing should follow words, not characters,
so the hand-set shifts can go.

214
00:11:57,843 --> 00:12:00,126
And shared parts
would shrink the scene code.

215
00:12:04,533 --> 00:12:05,589
Third, subtitles,

216
00:12:05,589 --> 00:12:09,440
the biggest source of review findings
in the Chinese versions.

217
00:12:09,760 --> 00:12:13,194
Hand-written rules decide
where each subtitle line breaks.

218
00:12:13,514 --> 00:12:17,196
The first rewrite of those rules
fixed most of its targets,

219
00:12:17,196 --> 00:12:19,317
but made 11 other subtitles worse.

220
00:12:19,637 --> 00:12:24,672
It took three more rounds to fix those,
so next time, the test cases come first.

221
00:12:24,992 --> 00:12:28,661
A grammar tool could suggest the breaks,
and the rules could check them.

222
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:32,835
Last, and closest to the user's vision:
interactivity.

223
00:12:33,155 --> 00:12:36,224
The first two videos each come
with a playground,

224
00:12:36,224 --> 00:12:40,984
a small web page to try the idea yourself,
but it sits apart from the video.

225
00:12:41,304 --> 00:12:44,246
Next, pausing the video
should open it right there,

226
00:12:44,246 --> 00:12:46,552
in the same state, and keep your answer.

227
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:51,536
Want to try this yourself?

228
00:12:51,856 --> 00:12:54,096
A human picks the learner and the question.

229
00:12:54,416 --> 00:12:56,336
Write the script first, as code.

230
00:12:56,656 --> 00:12:58,256
Let the audio be the clock.

231
00:12:58,576 --> 00:13:02,096
Use independent reviewers,
including a simulated viewer.

232
00:13:02,416 --> 00:13:05,467
Measure what you can't perceive,
and check the fixes too.

233
00:13:05,787 --> 00:13:07,536
And keep every step in a file.

234
00:13:07,783 --> 00:13:09,447
Above all, remember the goal.

235
00:13:09,767 --> 00:13:13,671
As the user put it,
AI should not just be cognitive offloading.

236
00:13:13,991 --> 00:13:18,194
It should make knowledge easier to reach,
and still let you learn just as deeply.

237
00:13:18,433 --> 00:13:19,521
One last thing.

238
00:13:19,841 --> 00:13:23,808
This video was made the same way,
so it has the same blind spot:

239
00:13:23,808 --> 00:13:26,412
the agent that made it can't listen to it.

